  "untestable": 0.1,
  "artifact": 0.05,
  "uncertain": 0.05
 },
 "decision": "proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The causal effect of a teammate DRIVE immediately preceding a PNR (within the same possession) on the PNR's Points Per Possession (PPP), holding other factors constant. Specifically, E[PNR PPP | DRIVE preceding PNR] - E[PNR PPP | No DRIVE preceding PNR].",
  "unit": "The PNR play-type possession. Each observation is a single PNR play, characterized by its initiator, screener, and outcome.",
  "sample": "All PNR play-type possessions from NBA regular season and playoff games for the 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 seasons (up to the date of data availability). Possessions will be filtered to include only those where a clear sequence of actions can be identified from the derived play-type records. This includes possessions where a DRIVE by a teammate (excluding the PNR initiator) occurs immediately prior to the PNR, and possessions where a PNR occurs without such a preceding DRIVE.",
  "power": "A minimal detectable effect size will be calculated based on the observed variance of PNR PPP and the expected sample sizes for both treatment (DRIVE preceding PNR) and control (no DRIVE preceding PNR) groups. We will aim for 80% power to detect a 0.05 PPP difference at an alpha level of 0.05. If the sample size for the 'DRIVE preceding PNR' group is too small (e.g., fewer than 1000 observations), the study will be deemed underpowered for individual player analysis, but may still proceed for aggregate league-wide analysis.",
  "controls": "Statistical control variables will include: game state (score differential, quarter/period), time on shot clock at PNR initiation, PNR initiator, PNR screener, opposing defense (e.g., identity of defenders, defensive scheme if available), team offense, and season. Player-specific and team-specific fixed effects will be considered to account for unobserved heterogeneity. Propensity score matching or inverse probability weighting will be explored to balance observable confounders if direct regression controls are insufficient.",
  "falsification": "A falsification test will involve examining whether a teammate DRIVE *after* a PNR (within the same possession, which would be nonsensical for the hypothesis) shows a similar spurious effect on PNR PPP. We will also check if other preceding play types that are not expected to generate the hypothesized effect (e.g., an ISO or HANDOFF) show a similar 'boost' to PNR PPP, which would suggest a more general sequence effect rather than a specific DRIVE effect.",
  "multiplicity": "No multiple comparisons are planned beyond the primary hypothesis test. If exploratory analyses are conducted (e.g., by player, team, or specific defensive matchups), these will be clearly separated and labeled as exploratory, with appropriate adjustments for multiple testing (e.g., Bonferroni correction or FDR) if formal inference is attempted.",
  "decision_rule": "The hypothesis will be considered supported if the estimated coefficient for 'DRIVE preceding PNR' is positive and statistically significant (p < 0.05) in a well-controlled regression model, and if falsification tests do not reveal alternative explanations. The magnitude of the effect will also be considered for practical significance.",
  "data_version": "Derived play-type possession records from the graph database, incorporating play-by-play data, for NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25 (complete), and 2025-26 (partial, up to 2026-06-14) seasons. The 'DRIVE' play type, as defined in the glossary, will be identified using the most recent classification algorithms."
 }
}

